# DispatchSEO self-hosted (docker compose) environment.
#   cp .env.docker.example .env
# Only CRON_SECRET is required - everything else has a sane default or is
# configured later in the dashboard's setup wizard.

# --- REQUIRED ---
# Auth token the cron container uses to trigger scheduled jobs. Generate:
#   openssl rand -hex 24
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Optional ---
# Password for the bundled Postgres. Only set this BEFORE the first boot -
# it is baked into the database volume at creation, and changing it later
# requires wiping the volume (docker compose down -v deletes all data).
# start.sh generates a random one on a fresh install (no database volume
# yet); installs created before that keep the old default until rotated.
# Postgres is only reachable from the app, on an internal-only network.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# The URL your instance is reached at. start.sh fills this in for a local
# trial. With the bundled builder (an agent credential below) the whole
# pipeline runs in-stack and localhost is fine forever; only set a public
# URL if you skip the builder and rely on GitHub-hosted workflow schedules
# instead, which must be able to reach this URL.
# APP_URL=

# --- The in-stack builder (automatic content builds, no public URL) ---
# Your Claude Code OAuth token - the builder runs your agent headlessly on
# your existing Claude subscription (same BYO model as the cloud path).
# EASIEST: skip this and paste the token on the dashboard's "Turn on automatic
# builds" step - it's stored encrypted and the builder picks it up, no file to
# edit. Setting it here still works and OVERRIDES the dashboard value (handy
# for scripted/headless installs). To get the token, run on your own machine:
#   claude setup-token
# and paste the sk-ant-oat... token here, no line breaks. Without it (and with
# nothing pasted on the dashboard) the builder idles and scheduled builds
# don't run.
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# Running Codex instead? Set this rather than the token above, and switch the
# project to Codex on the dashboard's Settings page. Several can be set at
# once: the agent is a per-PROJECT choice, so a stack hosting two sites can
# run a different agent on each, and the builder is handed the right
# credential per job. Unlike a
# Claude subscription this is metered - OpenAI bills you per run. Create a key
# at https://platform.openai.com/api-keys on an account with credit on it.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Which model Codex builds use (default: gpt-5). Model availability is
# per-account - if your key 404s on the default, set one your account can
# serve. The GitHub-Actions pipeline's equivalent is the SEO_CODEX_MODEL
# repo variable.
# CODEX_MODEL=

# Running Cursor instead? Set this and switch the project to Cursor on the
# dashboard's Settings page - same per-PROJECT choice as above, so any mix
# of the three credentials can be set at once. Builds run on your Cursor
# plan; mint the key at cursor.com/dashboard/api - any plan can (the
# interactive `cursor-agent login` on your own machine never reaches the
# builder container - only a key does).
# CURSOR_API_KEY=

# Which model Cursor builds use (default: auto - Cursor picks). The
# GitHub-Actions pipeline's equivalent is the SEO_CURSOR_MODEL repo
# variable.
# CURSOR_MODEL=

# GitHub access for the builder (clone, push, open + merge PRs). Normally
# NOT needed here: the builder reuses the token you connect in the setup
# wizard's One-tap merge step. Set this only to override it.
# BUILDER_GH_TOKEN=

# How often the builder asks the backend whether anything is due, in
# seconds. Default 600 (10 minutes). Lower it if you want builds to start
# sooner after you approve something; the backend can also adjust it.
# BUILDER_POLL_SECONDS=600

# Host port the dashboard is published on (container always listens on
# 3000). Leave unset and start.sh picks 4005, or the next free port up if
# something already holds it. Set it to pin a specific port.
# DISPATCH_PORT=

# Host interface the dashboard is published on. Defaults to 127.0.0.1 -
# the dashboard is reachable from the machine running Docker and nowhere
# else, which is what you want: it holds your GitHub and Claude tokens.
# On a server, reach it either by giving it a domain (set DOMAIN below and
# run the `domain` profile - Caddy terminates HTTPS in front of it) or with
# an SSH tunnel: ssh -L 4005:localhost:4005 root@your-server-ip
# Only set this to 0.0.0.0 if you deliberately want it on your LAN AND you
# have a firewall in front of it. Note that Docker's published ports bypass
# ufw, so "I have ufw enabled" is not that firewall.
# DISPATCH_BIND=127.0.0.1

# The first visitor to /setup claims this instance. start.sh generates this
# token into .env and prints it when the stack boots; the /setup form asks for
# it. Without one, /setup only accepts a claim from the machine itself.
# SETUP_TOKEN=

# --- Optional overrides (the setup wizard handles all of these) ---
# DASHBOARD_PASSWORD=
# MCP_API_KEY=
# GSC_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=
# GSC_SITE_URL=
# DATAFORSEO_LOGIN=
# DATAFORSEO_PASSWORD=
# GH_MERGE_TOKEN=
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# ALERT_EMAIL=
# ALERT_EMAIL_FROM=
# CUSTOMER_EMAIL_FROM=

# Extra starting points for the weekly keyword research, comma-separated
# (e.g. "time tracking,invoicing for freelancers"). Optional: research
# already seeds itself from your tracked keywords and your top Search
# Console queries. Use this to push it toward topics you don't rank for yet.
# SEED_KEYWORDS=

# Force building the app + builder images from this clone's source instead
# of pulling the prebuilt ones from GHCR. Set this if you forked the repo
# and changed code - otherwise start.sh prefers the prebuilt images when
# they're pullable, and your local changes would not run.
# BUILD_FROM_SOURCE=1

# Once a day this install sends dispatchseo.com two things: a random id
# generated on your machine at first boot (DISPATCH_INSTALL_ID, written into
# this file below the secrets), and which version you're running. That's the
# entire payload - no domain, no email, no keywords, no site data, no tokens.
# It exists because there is otherwise no way to tell how many people actually
# run DispatchSEO: downloads count attempts, and one person re-running
# start.sh looks identical to ten new installs.
# Set this to off to stop it. Nothing else changes - no nag, no missing
# features, and you can confirm it took effect with:
#   docker compose exec app wget -qO- --header "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET" \
#     http://localhost:3000/api/cron/heartbeat
# DISPATCHSEO_TELEMETRY=off

# Put the dashboard on your own domain with automatic HTTPS. Point the
# domain's DNS A record at this machine, set this, and re-run start.sh -
# the bundled Caddy proxy handles certificates and renewal by itself.
# Leave unset if you run your own reverse proxy (then set APP_URL above).
# DOMAIN=dispatch.example.com
